Sure! To sort your `myItemList` so that all items with a status of `'urgent'` appear first, followed by the rest sorted alphabetically by name in ascending order, you can use the `prioritizeAndSort` function like this:

```javascript
// Assuming myItemList is defined somewhere above:
const myItemList = [
  { name: 'Alpha', status: 'normal' },
  { name: 'Bravo', status: 'urgent' },
  { name: 'Charlie', status: 'normal' },
  { name: 'Delta', status: 'urgent' }
];

// Call the function
const sortedList = prioritizeAndSort({
  items: JSON.stringify(myItemList),
  priorityStatus: 'urgent',
  ascending: true
});

console.log(sortedList);
```

This will output:

```json
[
  {"name":"Bravo","status":"urgent"},
  {"name":"Delta","status":"urgent"},
  {"name":"Alpha","status":"normal"},
  {"name":"Charlie","status":"normal"}
]
```

If you already have `myItemList` defined, just replace the example array with your own data. Let me know if you'd like help integrating this into your existing code!
